End an s command's flags at a newline.
A newline ends a sed command, but the s flag parser skipped it with the
other blanks and went on reading flags from the next line. A newline
follows the flags when the script line ends in a backslash, as in
"sed -n -e 's/a/b/\' -e p": the p was taken as a flag, so only changed
lines were printed, and "-e g" in its place made the substitution global
instead of running the g command. The same happened to the line after a
w flag's file name. A # comment after the flags swallowed all following
lines, though a comment elsewhere ends at the newline. Stop the flags,
and such a comment, at a newline, so the next line is parsed as a
command.

---
 editors/sed.c |    8 ++++----
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -481,8 +481,8 @@
 			}
 			continue;
 		}
-		/* Skip spaces */
-		if (isspace(substr[idx]))
+		/* Skip spaces, but not a newline, which ends the command */
+		if (substr[idx] != '\n' && isspace(substr[idx]))
 			continue;
 
 		switch (substr[idx]) {
@@ -511,10 +511,10 @@
 			break;
 		/* Comment */
 		case '#':
-			// while (substr[++idx]) continue;
-			idx += strlen(substr + idx); // same
+			idx += strcspn(substr + idx, "\n");
 			/* Fall through */
 		/* End of command */
+		case '\n':
 		case ';':
 		case '}':
 			goto out;
